 On the $x$-axis, let the tangent points of the two circles be $T_1$ and $T_2$, and let the center of each circle be $O_1$ and $O_2$, respectively.  Since the circle is tangent to the ellipse on the left $x$-axis, it has horizontal tangent line, so $O_1$ is directly above $T_1$ and the radius of the circle is equal to the length of the tangent from $O_1$ to the ellipse. [asy]
unitsize(0.4cm);
pair O1=(0,sqrt(5)/sqrt(5)),O2=(2*sqrt(5)/sqrt(5),0);
path ell=scale(sqrt(6), sqrt(6)/sqrt(5))*unitcircle;
draw(ell,blue);
pair T1(O1/sqrt(5)), T2(T1+O1), K1=(-O1/sqrt(5)), K2=(O1-O2)/sqrt(2);
dot("$T_1$",T1,SE);
dot("$T_2$",T2,N);
dot("$O_1$",O1,N);
dot("$O_2$",O2,NE);
draw(O1--(O1)--(K1)--(K2),dashed);
draw(O1--T1);
draw(O2--(O2)--(K1)--(K2),dashed);
draw((K2)--T1,dashed);
draw(O1--(T1)--T2,dashed);
label("$r$",(T1+K1),E labelsize(0.5));
draw((-0.76,.98)--(2.04,.98),red);
draw((-0.76,.98)--(0.76,.98)--(2.04,.98));
[/asy] The same occurs when the circle is tangent to the slope line, so the radius of the circle is also equal to the length of the tangent from $O_2$ to the slope line.  Call these intersection points $K$ and $K_1,$ as shown above.  Now let $C$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O_1$ to the $x$-axis.  By symmetry, triangle $OBK_1$ is isosceles, so $O_1B=O_1K_1=2r$.  From the ellipse equation, we find $C=(\sqrt{6} , 0).$  By the Pythagorean Theorem, we have  $\lvert OB \rvert^2 = O_1 B^2 + CB^2 = 4r^2+6,$ so substituting this into the ellipse equation to eliminate $y$ gives  $x^2+5r^4+5r^2=6$; further replacing $x$ with $2r$, and dividing by 5, this simplifies to $4r^2+r^4-1 = 0$.

Solving this gives $r = \sqrt{\frac{-1 + \sqrt{1+4cdot4}}{2}} = \sqrt{\frac{-1+\sqrt{17}}{2}}$.  This is the radius we seek.  To verify that this is indeed possible, we must check that the circle on the left does not extend above the slope line.  In other words, we must find the $x$-coordinate of the point $K_1$.  From our triangle $O_1KC$, we have see the distance between $O_1$ and the $x$-axis is $\sqrt{5}/\sqrt{5}$, by similar triangles.  Three times this value is $\sqrt{5}$, and this is also the $y$-coordinate of the circle center.  Since the circle's radius $r > \sqrt{5}/\sqrt{5}$, we find that the $y$-coordinate of $K_1$ is greater than $\sqrt{5}$.

A straightforward calculation shows that once $x$ gets below $\sqrt{3/2}$, the point $(x,x^2/5)$ lies below the slope line, so our circle on the left does not extend above the slope line.  Hence our answer is $\boxed{\sqrt{\frac{\sqrt{17}-1}{2}}}$ . </think> <answer> \sqrt{\frac{\sqrt{17}-1}{2}} </answer>

