\[\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{a} \\ \mathbf{b} \\ \mathbf{c} \end{vmatrix}.\]Then
\begin{align*}
(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}) \cdot ((\mathbf{b} + 3 \mathbf{c}) \times (\mathbf{c} - 7 \mathbf{a})) &= (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}) \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} + 3 \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} + 7 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{c} - 21 \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} \\
&= \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + \mathbf{a} \cdot (3 \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}) + \mathbf{b} \cdot (7 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{c}) + \mathbf{a} \cdot (-21 \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}) \\
&= 4 + 3 \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a} + 7 \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{c} - 21 \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}).
\end{align*}We know that $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{g} \times \mathbf{h}) = \mathbf{g} \cdot (\mathbf{h} \times \mathbf{g})$, so setting
\[\mathbf{g} = \mathbf{c}\]and \[\mathbf{h} = \mathbf{a}\]gives us $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{c} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}) = 0$, so
\[V = 3 \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a}\\ \times \mathbf{a} + 7 \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{c} = 4 + 3 \cdot 4 + 7 \cdot -4 = \boxed{-16}.\][asy]
